~ exploration 
Stranger: So... [wait=0.8]it is true after all.
Stranger: The last god vanished centuries ago. No warning. No sign. One day the world simply grew quieter.
Stranger: For a long time, we did not know if another would ever come.
Stranger: Yet here you are.
Father Spirit: I am called the Father Spirit.
Father Spirit: I watch over families, neighbors, and communities — the small bonds that keep the world from falling apart.
Father Spirit: Tell me, young god…
Father Spirit: Do you know why you are here?
- I want to understand.
	Father Spirit: Good. Curiosity keeps power from becoming arrogance.
	Father Spirit: The truth is… none of us know why the last god disappeared.
	Father Spirit: That mystery has lingered like a storm that never quite arrived.
	Father Spirit: And now you stand where they once did.
	- Then we should find out what happened.
		Father Spirit: Yes. That question should have been asked long ago.
		Father Spirit: Perhaps you will be the one brave enough to ask it.
	- Does the world still need gods?
		Father Spirit: Some would say no.
		Father Spirit: But I have seen what happens when guidance disappears for too long.
	- That sounds dangerous.
		Father Spirit: It probably is.
		Father Spirit: Most important things are.
- If the world needs a god, I’ll help.
	Father Spirit: Helping the world is a heavier task than most gods expect.
	Father Spirit: People are stubborn. Communities fragile. Trust… easily broken.
	- Then I’ll protect them.
		Father Spirit: Protection is good.
		Father Spirit: Just remember. people must learn to stand too.
	- Someone has to try.
		Father Spirit: That may be the wisest thing you have said.
	- I didn’t say it would be easy.
		Father Spirit: Good.
		Father Spirit: You may survive this role after all.
- I don’t even know what I am.
	Father Spirit: Then you are exactly what I expected.
	Father Spirit: A god who arrives certain of everything is usually trouble.
	- Will you help me?
		Father Spirit: I can guide you.
		Father Spirit: But the path will still be yours.
	- So I really am a god?
		Father Spirit: The world seems to think so.
		Father Spirit: And the world rarely makes that mistake lightly.
	- What happened to the last one?
		Father Spirit: That is the question that has haunted us for centuries.
- How do you know I’m really a god?
	Father Spirit: When the last god disappeared… the world felt empty.
	Father Spirit: When you arrived… something shifted again.
	Father Spirit: Spirits notice these things.
	- So you’re guessing
		Father Spirit: All wisdom begins as an educated guess.
	- Then prove it.
		Father Spirit: Power will reveal itself soon enough.
	- I didn’t ask for this.
		Father Spirit: Neither did the last one.
- Maybe the world will change now.
	Father Spirit: Change is easy.
	Father Spirit: Changing things without breaking them… that is difficult.
	- Sometimes things need to break.
		Father Spirit: That is what every reckless ruler has said.
	- I’ll be careful.
		Father Spirit: Good. Then perhaps this age will survive you.
	- You sound worried.
		Father Spirit: I have watched centuries of mistakes.
		Father Spirit: Worry comes naturally.
Father Spirit: Whether you are ready or not, the world has begun moving again.
Father Spirit: Communities will look to you.
Father Spirit: Some with hope. Others with fear.
Father Spirit: I will watch. I will guide when I can.
Father Spirit: But understand this, young god.
Father Spirit: The last one disappeared without warning.
Father Spirit: So let us both hope history is not repeating itself.
Father Spirit: Here... Have this.
Father Spirit: Consider it a token of friendship.#
